	<logo>https://static.globalissues.org/i/globalissues/logo-feed.jpg</logo><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43934</id><title>Zambia: An Uneven Contest, an Easy Win</title><updated>2026-08-31T10:42:04-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43934" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Partial-presidential_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Partial-presidential_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Partial-presidential_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Partial-presidential_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
LONDON, August 31 (IPS)  - On 24 August, police &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cgrvjn88rxqo&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;sealed off&lt;/a&gt; access to Zambia’s courts, closed the day before the deadline for petitions to challenge the results of the 13 August presidential election. The judiciary’s top official cited security reasons, but the timing left little doubt that authorities meant to block the defeated candidate’s legal challenge.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43934&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Zambia: An Uneven Contest, an Easy Win”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43933</id><title>Africa’s Pastoralists Need More Than COP17’s $1.2 billion Rangelands Pledge</title><updated>2026-08-31T10:24:01-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43933" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/ENB-IISD-Anastasia-Rodopoulou-UNCCD-COP17-28Aug2026-Photo-127_1-1.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/ENB-IISD-Anastasia-Rodopoulou-UNCCD-COP17-28Aug2026-Photo-127_1-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/ENB-IISD-Anastasia-Rodopoulou-UNCCD-COP17-28Aug2026-Photo-127_1-1-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/ENB-IISD-Anastasia-Rodopoulou-UNCCD-COP17-28Aug2026-Photo-127_1-1.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
DAR ES SALAAM, August 31 (IPS)  - When the rains fail across Tanzania’s northern Maasai Steppe, herders drive their cattle farther in search of pasture and water.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43933&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Africa’s Pastoralists Need More Than COP17’s $1.2 billion Rangelands Pledge”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43932</id><title>Beyond Resilience: Climate Change and the Rights of Char Dwellers in Bangladesh</title><updated>2026-08-31T08:41:08-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43932" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Char-residents_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Char-residents_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Char-residents_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/Char-residents_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
DHAKA, Bangladesh, August 31 (IPS)  - Bangladesh’s riverine and coastal chars have always been landscapes of movement, but climate change is making that movement more violent, frequent, and unequal. The shifting islands of the Jamuna, Brahmaputra, Padma, and other rivers now reveal how climate stress multiplies deprivation, weakens citizenship, and forces char dwellers to absorb the costs of a crisis they did little to create.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43932&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Beyond Resilience: Climate Change and the Rights of Char Dwellers in Bangladesh”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43931</id><title>35 Years After Semipalatinsk, Kazakhstan’s Nuclear Choice Still Challenges the World</title><updated>2026-08-31T08:21:47-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43931" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/35th_1.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/35th_1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/35th_1-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/35th_1.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
TOKYO / ASTANA, August 31 (IPS)  - Across the vast steppe of northeastern Kazakhstan, the scars of the Cold War remain visible. At the Semipalatinsk Nuclear Test Site, the Soviet Union conducted 456 nuclear tests between 1949 and 1989, exposing nearby communities to radiation and leaving health and environmental consequences that have endured across generations.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43931&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “35 Years After Semipalatinsk, Kazakhstan’s Nuclear Choice Still Challenges the World”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43937</id><title>Nepal flooding deaths surpass 900 as needs climb</title><updated>2026-08-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43937" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/28-08-2026-UNICEF-Nepal-floods-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/28-08-2026-UNICEF-Nepal-floods-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/28-08-2026-UNICEF-Nepal-floods-02.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/28-08-2026-UNICEF-Nepal-floods-02.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The confirmed death toll from Nepal’s catastrophic floods has surged to 903, with more than 4,200 still missing as rescue crews and humanitarian workers battle blocked highways to reach the hardest-hit areas, UN Spokesperson Stéphane Dujarric said on Monday.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43937&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Nepal flooding deaths surpass 900 as needs climb”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43936</id><title>‘Slavery is not a thing of the past’: UN supports call for reparations</title><updated>2026-08-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43936" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/31-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Kansas-USA.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/31-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Kansas-USA.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/31-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Kansas-USA.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/31-08-2026-Adobe-Stock-Kansas-USA.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Slavery is not just a thing of the past. Its effects continue to be felt in systemic inequality and racism. This was reiterated on Monday by the UN Committee against Racial Discrimination (CERD) as the world marks the International Day of People of African Descent. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43936&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Slavery is not a thing of the past’: UN supports call for reparations”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43935</id><title>‘Every step towards disarmament matters,’ nuclear testing survivors warn</title><updated>2026-08-31T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43935" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/31-08-2026_Nuclear%20Disarment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/31-08-2026_Nuclear%20Disarment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/31-08-2026_Nuclear%20Disarment.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/31-08-2026_Nuclear%20Disarment.jpg/image1170x530cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;He was born without arms, a legacy of four decades of nuclear explosions at the Semipalatinsk test site in his native Kazakhstan.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/31/43935&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘Every step towards disarmament matters,’ nuclear testing survivors warn”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/30/43930</id><title>‘The lake is our life’: Protecting a shared lifeline in Uganda</title><updated>2026-08-30T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/30/43930" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/26-08-2026_Uganda_Lake_Albert_fishing_nets.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/26-08-2026_Uganda_Lake_Albert_fishing_nets.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/26-08-2026_Uganda_Lake_Albert_fishing_nets.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Production%20Library/2026/08/26-08-2026_Uganda_Lake_Albert_fishing_nets.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On the shores of Nkondo, in Uganda’s Kikuube district, the day begins before sunrise. Fishers enter the lake to fish, while families around Lake Albert rely on its waters for food, income and daily survival.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/30/43930&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “‘The lake is our life’: Protecting a shared lifeline in Uganda”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/29/43929</id><title>Rising seas, rising stakes: What increasing sea-levels mean to humanity</title><updated>2026-08-29T05:00:00-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/29/43929" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/25-08-2026-UNDP-Papua-New-Guinea.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" /><media:content url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/25-08-2026-UNDP-Papua-New-Guinea.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/25-08-2026-UNDP-Papua-New-Guinea.jpg/image100x100cropped.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://global.unitednations.entermediadb.net/assets/mediadb/services/module/asset/downloads/preset/Collections/Embargoed/2026/08/25-08-2026-UNDP-Papua-New-Guinea.jpg/image1440x560cropped.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The ocean is rising faster now than at any point in recorded history. The impacts are already being felt and the threats are growing.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/29/43929&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Rising seas, rising stakes: What increasing sea-levels mean to humanity”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry><entry><id>https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/28/43922</id><title>Uganda Announces End of Ebola Outbreak, Yet Regional Risks Remain</title><updated>2026-08-28T16:50:26-07:00</updated><link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/28/43922" /><link rel="enclosure" type="image/jpeg" href="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/briefing-reporters-outside_.jpg" /><media:content url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/briefing-reporters-outside_.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" /><media:thumbnail url="https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/briefing-reporters-outside_-100x100.jpg" width="100" height="100" /><summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://static.globalissues.org/ips/2026/08/briefing-reporters-outside_.jpg&quot; width=&quot;640&quot; alt=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;
UNITED NATIONS, August 28 (IPS)  - Following 42 consecutive days without a new confirmed case, The Africa Centres for Disease Control and Prevention (Africa CDC) and the World Health Organization (WHO) have announced the end of the Ebola outbreak in Uganda.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.globalissues.org/news/2026/08/28/43922&quot;&gt;Read the full story, “Uganda Announces End of Ebola Outbreak, Yet Regional Risks Remain”, on globalissues.org&lt;/a&gt; →&lt;/p&gt;</summary></entry></feed><!-- 0.0019s ch:1 -->